WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.380
<v Maya>Welcome back to Singletrack Sessions. Today we're talking about trail building, and I'm joined by Liam, trail builder for the Cascadia Alliance. Liam, thanks for being here.</v>

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.140
<v Liam>Thanks, Maya. Excited for this one — trail building is something I've been thinking about a lot.</v>

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:27.780
<v Maya>Before we dive in, a quick word from our sponsor Strava. Strava is the home for athletes — track every ride, run, and swim, and compare segments with friends. Use code POD for an extended trial.</v>

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.200
<v Maya>Let's start there. What's the most underrated piece of trail building?</v>

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:50.160
<v Liam>The short answer is Drainage is the single highest-leverage design choice on any trail. If water leaves on its own, the trail will last a decade. We've seen this play out at teams using Spoke &amp; Wrench. Once you internalize that, the rest of the decisions get easier.</v>

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.920
<v Maya>Push back on the conventional wisdom for me. What's the most underrated piece of trail building?</v>

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:09.240
<v Liam>I'd put it this way: The most common mistake new builders make is grading too aggressively — gentle is durable, steep is romantic. The exception proves the rule — there are edge cases, but they're rarer than people assume.</v>

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.820
<v Maya>I want to dig into that. What's something you used to believe about this that you've revised?</v>

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:19.200
<v Maya>That's it for today's episode of Singletrack Sessions. See you next time.</v>
